diagnosis_text:
I investigated the core triangle (router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3) for true link instability. The L2/L1 side is clean: all core interfaces report Link detected: yes at 10G, no errors/drops, and only two carrier changes since boot on each core-facing port (router_core_[1–3]: /sys/class/net/eth[0–1]/carrier_changes = 2), consistent with initial bring-up, not ongoing flaps. Application checks from the edge (HTTP curls from host_1_1_1_1 to all web servers) are fast and consistent, and a topology-wide ping matrix shows 0% loss.

What likely looked like “links bouncing” was OSPF adjacency churn during FRR startup a few minutes ago. FRR logs on all three cores show OSPF neighbor negotiations around 16:58 (e.g., “Packet[DD]: Neighbor … Negotiation done”), and each OSPF neighbor currently shows State Full with only six state changes and the most recent change ~1–3 minutes ago. SPF has been idle since, and dead timers are counting down normally. No MTU/timer mismatches are present.

Localization: OSPF on the core interlinks (rc1-rc2 eth0, rc1-rc3 eth1/eth0, rc2-rc3 eth1/eth1). Mechanism: a one-time FRR reload/DR election/LSDB sync produced adjacency state transitions that operations dashboards can present as “core links down/up.”

Root cause: a transient FRR OSPF restart/reload caused temporary OSPF neighbor re-establishment, not physical link flaps. Corrective fix: none required now; the core is steady. For extra steadiness on /31 Ethernet point-to-point links, set OSPF network type to point-to-point to avoid DR elections, and ensure FRR isn’t being reloaded by automation. 
=== END ===